    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d88">

                <pb facs="0236" n="140" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg140"/>

                <head>No. 88.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hurlbut to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Bogota</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1871-10-07"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 7, 1871</date>.
                        (Received October 30.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 63.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I am just informed by the secretary of
                    foreign relations that the commandant of the port of Colon (Aspinwall) required
                    bonds from the captain and agents of the American steamer Virginius, not to
                    leave that port, which order the President had immediately disavowed and
                    annulled.</p>
                <p>I expressed at once the thanks of the United States for the promptness-with which
                    this had been done, without any solicitation on our part, for I had no knowledge
                    of the matter in any form. For some reason I have received no communication on
                    the subject from Consul Perry. It is probably on the way and will arrive by next
                    mail.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S. A. HURLBUT,</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
